Gate B1 of Task 3 -- A BROWSER CLIENT JOINED A REAL ARMAGETRON SERVER.

THE DIRECTORY IS b1/ AND THE VERDICT IN THE LOG IS CALLED B2. Not a mismatch
to fix: the task brief numbered its two new gates B1 and B2, and by the time
they were written web/tools/bridge-gate.steps already had B0, B1 and A1 from
Task 2. Renaming Task 2's B1 would have falsified the labels in
docs/evidence/m-a-bridge/task2/, so the verdicts continue the sequence as B2
and B3 while the directories keep the brief's names. b1/ = the join, b2/ =
the round.

WHAT WAS RUN, exactly, from the repo root of this worktree:

  docker build -f bridge/test-server/Dockerfile -t aa-dedicated .
  docker run -d --name aa-server -p 4534:4534/udp aa-dedicated
  node bridge/relay.mjs --port 8010 --allow-private > relay.log 2>&1 &
  python3 -m http.server 8008 --directory web/dist-m1 &
  node web/tools/drive-browser.mjs --headed --out /tmp/bridge-gate \
       --url 'http://localhost:8008/armagetronad.html?bridge=ws://127.0.0.1:8010' \
       --script-file web/tools/bridge-gate.steps
  docker logs aa-server > server.log 2>&1

`docker run` WITHOUT --rm, deliberately. The committed recipe in
bridge-gate.steps uses --rm; this run did not, because an earlier run's
container exited part way through and --rm had already deleted it along with
its log. See the note in bridge-gate.steps headed "IF B2 TIMES OUT".

THE FIVE VERDICTS, counted the way the steps file says to:

  $ grep -c '\[console.log\].*BRIDGEGATE.*"PASS":true' console.log
  5

  B0 configured-and-idle-until-asked                 PASS  (Task 2)
  B1 socket-open-and-bound                           PASS  (Task 2)
  A1 address-text-maps-the-way-the-game-maps-it      PASS  (Task 2)
  B2 reached-the-bookmarked-server-through-the-bridge PASS  <- this directory
  B3 a-full-round-completed-...                      PASS  <- ../b2/

B2's reading in full, from console.log:

  {"ws_readyState":1,"state":1,"live_handles":[3],"bound":[[3,1]],
   "sent":[[3,78]],"pending":0,
   "baseline":{"dropped":0,"sendFailures":0,"dataErrors":8},
   "dropped":0,"sendFailures":0,"dataErrors":8,
   "new_dropped":0,"new_sendFailures":0,"new_dataErrors":0,
   "lastDataError":"port 0 is outside the allowed range 4533-4599",
   "rosters":4,"rounds_started":1,"PASS":true}

The dataErrors of 8 come from the LAN walk and are present before this gate
begins, which is why the assertion is on the delta. They are NOT the
broadcast: nSocket::Broadcast returns -1 under __EMSCRIPTEN__ && !DEDICATED
(src/network/nSocket.cpp:2210) and never reaches the relay. They are the
unicast on the line above it in nServerInfo::GetFromLAN -- SendImmediately(0,
false) to peer 0, whose nAddress has no port because the LAN browse has no
server to point it at -- refused by bridge/policy.mjs against its 4533-4599
range. Correct on both sides, zero of them appear during either round
(new_dataErrors 0 at both B2 and B3), and the full argument is in the
baseline comment in bridge-gate.steps.

AND THE ASSERTION THAT ACTUALLY SETTLES IT, from server.log, which is the
server's own console and not the client's:

  [1] Received login from 192.168.215.1:41554 via socket *.*.*.*:4534,
      network version: 0.2.9_alpha (ID: 17).
  [1] web_user entered the game.

  $ grep -c 'Received login' server.log
  1

EXACTLY ONE LOGIN IS THE POINT OF THAT COUNT, not a formality. Docker
Desktop's UDP proxy sits between the relay's socket and the server, and the
source endpoint it presents is NOT the relay's: the relay bound udp port
58639 (relay.log) and the server saw 192.168.215.1:41554. So the proxy does
rewrite the source. What it does not do on this machine is rewrite it
INCONSISTENTLY: a server identifies a client by source ip:port, and a proxy
that picked a new port per datagram would show up here as a stream of logins
from ascending ports, each treated as a fresh client. One login, one player,
one completed round. Re-measure this on any other host before assuming it;
the fallback is a native dedicated build with no Docker in the path.

THE MENU ROUTE IS IN menu-discovery/. It was predicted from the constructor
order in net_game() (src/tron/gGame.cpp:2397) and gServerFavorites.cpp, then
confirmed screen by screen with the throwaway script in that directory:
05 is the network menu as it opens (on Online Multiplayer), 06-09 are one
Down each, and 09 shows Server Bookmarks selected -- FOUR Downs from the top.
10 is the bookmark list, which opens already on "Connect to 127.0.0.1", so
one further Enter connects and no Down is needed there.

The gate itself uses THREE Downs, not four, and both numbers are right: it
arrives at the network menu with LAN Multiplayer still selected, because the
Task 2 walk above it entered LAN and came back. 10-*.png and 11-*.png in this
directory are that route as the gate actually walked it.
